{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788351176980,"sessionID":"ses_f9df6b623ffeAF0sWStYNv5qi5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_56e4185e44d444c7982d202a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-cannbench-kernel\">\n# Skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel\n\n# pyasc v2 CANNBench kernel workflow\n\nUse this skill only for the vendored benchmark under\n`integrations/cannbench/`. Its compact module contract overrides the generic\nstandalone-kernel workflow.\n\n## Non-negotiable contract\n\n- Treat `tasks/<op>/proto.yaml`, `golden.py`, and all 20 entries in\n  `cases.yaml` as one indivisible specification.\n- Produce one `candidate.py` with the exact top-level callable and defaults.\n- Use `torch` only for allocation, metadata views, and launches. Numerical work\n  belongs in one or more `@asc2.jit` kernels.\n- Import and call `ensure_npu_platform` from `._pyasc_runtime`.\n- Target `Ascend950PR_9599`, at most 72 vector cores, and UB capacity 253952 B.\n- For the pinned runtime use `asc2.global_tensor`, `asc2.copy_in`, and\n  `asc2.copy_out`; use `real_shape` for tails. Do not substitute the older\n  `tensor/load/store` spelling.\n- A production candidate normally uses bare `@asc2.jit` or an evidenced\n  allocation option. `always_compile=True` is a development aid, not a\n  submission requirement.\n\n## Required workflow\n\n1. Read the three task files and summarize every dtype, rank, attribute, list\n   arity, special value, and boundary shape. Do not optimize for one example.\n2. Read [operator patterns](references/operator-patterns.md), selecting only\n   the target operator section.\n3. Design host dispatch, kernel specializations, tails, dtype conversions, UB\n   budget, and numerical behavior before writing code.\n4. Implement the exact callable. Run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` and\n   the worker static contract check.\n5. Run the exact-v2 local compile gate for that operator as described in\n   [local validation](references/local-validation.md). A compile failure is\n   measured feedback; repair it and re-run all 20 cases.\n6. Have a different available model review the task, design, candidate, and\n   local report. Record both model IDs, OpenCode session IDs, loaded skill\n   source hashes, prompt/design/candidate hashes, and gate results.\n\nNever claim numerical correctness or performance from the local compile gate.\nOnly camodel execution can provide local numerical evidence; only CANNBench on\nthe real NPU is the acceptance/performance oracle.\n\n## Evidence labels\n\nUse exactly one label for every conclusion:\n\n- `verified-local-compile`: all task routes lower through pinned pyasc v2.\n- `verified-camodel`: outputs were executed and compared to the task golden.\n- `verified-cannbench`: official hardware result and case count are recorded.\n- `suspected`: evidence is incomplete or the root cause is inferred.\n\nDo not promote a locally qualified candidate into the canonical submission\npackage without numerical evidence. Do not spend a submission credit unless\nthe user explicitly enables remote evaluation.\n\n## Known limitations\n\nBefore using a workaround, consult\n[pyasc v2 blockers](references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md). Preserve full case\ncoverage when a case exposes a compiler limit; never remove or shrink the case\nto make a gate pass.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/local-validation.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel","dir":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel","time":{"start":1788351176967,"end":1788351176978}},"id":"prt_062096049001Q9IKN0Y1qLYx58","sessionID":"ses_f9df6b623ffeAF0sWStYNv5qi5","messageID":"msg_062094ab5001TsRcaI5YG3h5nf"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788351177379,"sessionID":"ses_f9df6b623ffeAF0sWStYNv5qi5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_62fe8e1f2d404359a582f384","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-syntax-constraints\">\n# Skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints\n\n# pyasc Syntax Constraints\n\nSyntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\nsource of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n`SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\nagainst that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n`asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n\n## Overview\n\npyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n\n## Supported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Example | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| Attribute access | `nums.__len__()` | |\n| `while` loop | `while i < n: ...` | |\n| `for` loop | `for i in asc2.range(n): ...` | Use `asc2.range()` inside kernel, not `range()` |\n| Binary operators | `a + b * c` | `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `//`, `**`, `&`, `\\|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` |\n| Unary operators | `~a`, `-a`, `not a` | |\n| `tuple` | `return x, y, z` | |\n| `list` | `nums = [a, b, c]` | |\n| `pass` | `pass` | |\n| Ternary `if` | `ans = x if x > 0 else 0` | |\n| `if`/`elif`/`else` | Full conditional blocks | |\n| Constants | `a = 1; b = True` | |\n| Comparisons | `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=` | |\n| Function calls | `func()` | Must be `@asc2.jit`-decorated or asc2 built-in |\n| Boolean logic | `and`, `or` | |\n| Augmented assign | `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=` | |\n| Assignment | `x = expr` | |\n| Annotated assign | `count: int = 3` | |\n| f-strings + assert | `assert cond, f\"msg {val}\"` | |\n| Subscript | `nums[0]` | |\n| Slice | `tensor[offset:]` | |\n\n## Unsupported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Status | Workaround |\n|--------|--------|------------|\n| Nested functions | Not supported | Define separate `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `global` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| `return` from kernel | Not supported | Kernels write to output tensors; device functions may return |\n| `return` inside `if`/`for` | Not supported | Use top-level return only in device functions |\n| `continue` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `print()` | Not supported | Use `assert` with f-strings |\n| `with open(...)` | Not supported | Do file I/O outside JIT |\n| `raise` | Not supported | Use `assert` instead |\n| `try`/`except` | Not supported | Handle errors outside JIT |\n| `yield` / `yield from` | Not supported | No generators |\n| `lambda` | Not supported | Define named functions |\n| `break` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `import` / `from...import` | Not supported | All imports outside JIT |\n| `async def` / `async with` | Not supported | No async |\n| `nonlocal` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| Class methods as kernels | Not supported | Use module-level functions |\n| If-scoped variables | Restricted | Initialize before `if`; use `asc.ConstExpr` for compile-time branching |\n| Unsigned int comparison | Restricted | Use `np.int64` or truthiness check instead |\n\n## Type Constraints\n\n### Kernel parameter types (runtime)\n\n| Supported | Not supported |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| `bool`, `int`, `float` | `str` |\n| `numpy` scalars and `ndarray` | `tuple`, `list`, `dict` |\n| `torch.Tensor` (NPU mode only) | Complex objects |\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` | |\n\n### Compile-time parameters\n\nUse `asc.ConstExpr[T]` for values that must be resolved at compile time:\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    # tile_size is resolved at compile time and included in the JIT cache key\n    ...\n```\n\n### Built-in whitelist inside JIT\n\nOnly these built-ins are available: `dict`, `float`, `int`, `isinstance`, `issubclass`, `len`, `list`, `range`, `repr`, `str`, `tuple`, `type`\n\nNote: For iteration inside `@asc2.jit`, use `asc2.range()` instead of `range()`.\nDo not assume optional keywords are portable between pyasc snapshots.\n\n### Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\n\n- **M/N/K tile loops are compile-time.** When the trip count is an\n  `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m/n/k tiles), a plain Python `for i in range(...)`\n  is fully unrolled at JIT time — that is the intended form for the loop that must\n  keep an operand resident in `L0A`/`L0B`. Use `asc2.range(...,\n  unroll_factor=2)` only on the *inner* tile loop you want software-pipelined\n  (double-buffered).\n- **Overlap (`gm_barrier=False`) doubles the pipelined L0 buffer.** The 2-deep\n  tile must fit half the L0 capacity (≤ 32 KiB for L0A/L0B, ≤ 64 KiB for the f32\n  L0C). A full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is 64 KiB and overflows when doubled\n  (`L0B overflow: 65536 bytes available, 131072 used`); shrink the tile (e.g.\n  `N_TILE=64`) before relying on overlap.\n- **Cube tile placement is explicit:** load/`asc2.copy` operands to\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L0A` (left) / `L0B` (right); stage from GM via\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L1` for reuse; the `@` result lands in L0C as f32 — cast with\n  `.to(asc2.float16)` before `asc2.store` if a 16-bit output is wanted.\n\n## Kernel vs Device Function Rules\n\n| Rule | Kernel function | Device function |\n|------|----------------|-----------------|\n| Launched from | Host via `kernel[core_num](...)` | Other `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `return` value | Not allowed | Allowed |\n| `@asc2.jit(...)` options | Effective (always_compile, etc.) | Ignored |\n| Nesting | Cannot be nested | Cannot be nested |\n\n## References\n\n- [Supported Syntax Details](references/supported-syntax.md)\n- [Unsupported Syntax Details](references/unsupported-syntax.md)\n- [Type Constraints](references/type-constraints.md)\n- [ConstExpr Guide](references/constexpr-guide.md)\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/supported-syntax.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/type-constraints.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/constexpr-guide.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/unsupported-syntax.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints","dir":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints","time":{"start":1788351177370,"end":1788351177377}},"id":"prt_0620961cc0012xkdtdYcu6uY5L","sessionID":"ses_f9df6b623ffeAF0sWStYNv5qi5","messageID":"msg_062094ab5001TsRcaI5YG3h5nf"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788351178163,"sessionID":"ses_f9df6b623ffeAF0sWStYNv5qi5","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_d3c61eb057ff444299c975f5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/rms_norm-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/rms_norm-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **RmsNorm**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `rms_norm` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # RmsNorm 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: 计算 RMS (均方根) 归一化。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - 大语言模型中的归一化层（LLaMA、Gemma 等使用 RMSNorm 替代 LayerNorm）\n19: - Transformer 架构中的预归一化（Pre-Norm）\n20: - 相比 LayerNorm 省去均值计算，推理效率更高\n21: \n22: **算子特征**：\n23: - 难度等级：L2（Normalization）\n24: - 双输入（x 和 gamma）单输出，涉及平方、均值、开方、除法、乘法等多步计算\n25: - 沿最后一维进行归一化，gamma 为可学习的缩放参数\n26: \n27: ## 2. 算子定义\n28: \n29: ### 数学公式\n30: \n31: **基本公式**：\n32: \n33: $$\n34: y = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{\\text{mean}(x^2) + \\epsilon}} \\cdot \\gamma\n35: $$\n36: \n37: 展开为：\n38: \n39: $$\n40: y_i = \\frac{x_i}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{D}\\sum_{j=1}^{D}x_j^2 + \\epsilon}} \\cdot \\gamma_i\n41: $$\n42: \n43: 其中：\n44: - `D` 为最后一维的大小（归一化维度）\n45: - `epsilon` 为数值稳定性参数，防止除零\n46: - `gamma` 为逐元素的缩放参数，shape 为 (D,)\n47: - 与 LayerNorm 不同，RMSNorm 不计算均值，也没有偏置（beta）参数\n48: \n49: ## 3. 接口规范\n50: \n51: ### 算子原型\n52: \n53: ```python\n54: cann_bench.rms_norm(Tensor x, Tensor gamma, float epsilon=1e-6) -> Tensor y\n55: ```\n56: \n57: ### 输入参数说明\n58: \n59: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n60: |------|------|--------|------|\n61: | x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量 |\n62: | gamma | Tensor | 必选 | 缩放参数，shape 为输入最后一维大小 |\n63: | epsilon | float | 1e-6 | 数值稳定性参数 |\n64: \n65: ### 输出\n66: \n67: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n68: |------|-------|-------|------|\n69: | y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | RMS 归一化后的张量 |\n70: \n71: ### 数据类型\n72: \n73: | x dtype | gamma dtype | 输出 dtype |\n74: |---------|------------|-----------|\n75: | float16 | float16 | float16 |\n76: | float32 | float32 | float32 |\n77: | bfloat16 | bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n78: \n79: ### 规则与约束\n80: \n81: - x 的 shape 为 (..., D)，gamma 的 shape 为 (D,)，其中 D 为最后一维大小\n82: - gamma 的 dtype 需与 x 一致\n83: - epsilon 为正数，通常取 1e-6 或 1e-5\n84: - 需注意数值稳定性：当输入值极小时，mean(x^2) 可能下溢；当输入值极大时，x^2 可能溢出\n85: \n86: ### 支持范围\n87: \n88: 输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n89: \n90: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n91: |---|---|---|\n92: | `x` 维度数 | 2 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 5 维 |\n93: | `D`（最后一维/归一化维度） | 1 ~ 16384 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 8192；`gamma` 的 shape 必须为 `(D,)` |\n94: | 前导维度乘积 `S = N0*N1*...` | 1 ~ 2097152 | cases.csv 实测 231 ~ 1000003 |\n95: | `gamma` 维度数 | 1 | 固定为 1 维 |\n96: | `epsilon` | 1e-12 ~ 1 | cases.csv 实测 1e-12 ~ 1e-3；须为正数，常用 1e-6 / 1e-5 |\n97: \n98: ## 4. 精度要求\n99: \n100: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n101: \n102: **误差指标**：\n103: \n104: 1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n105: \n106:    $$\n107:    \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n108:    $$\n109: \n110: 2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n111: \n112:    $$\n113:    \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n114:    $$\n115: \n116: **通过标准**：\n117: \n118: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n119: |----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n120: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n121: \n122: 当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n123: \n124: \n125: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n126: \n127: ```python\n128: import torch\n129: \n130: \"\"\"\n131: RmsNorm 算子 Torch Golden 参考实现\n132: \n133: 计算 RMS (均方根) 归一化\n134: \n135: 公式:\n136:     y = x / sqrt(mean(x^2) + eps) * gamma\n137: \n138: 参考 PyTorch API: torch.nn.functional.rms_norm\n139:     https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.rms_norm.html\n140: \n141: 参考论文: Root Mean Square Layer Normalization\n142:     https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07467\n143: \n144: Parameters:\n145:     - x: (..., D) 输入张量，最后一维为归一化维度\n146:     - gamma: (D,) 缩放参数\n147:     - epsilon: float, 默认 1e-6 - 数值稳定性参数\n148: \"\"\"\n149: \n150: \n151: def rms_norm(\n152:     x: torch.Tensor,\n153:     gamma: torch.Tensor,\n154:     epsilon: float = 1e-6\n155: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n156:     \"\"\"\n157:     计算 RMS (均方根) 归一化\n158: \n159:     Args:\n160:         x: 输入张量，shape (..., D)\n161:            最后一维 D 为归一化维度\n162:         gamma: 缩放参数，shape (D,)\n163:                与输入最后一维大小相同\n164:         epsilon: 数值稳定性参数，防止除零\n165:                  默认值 1e-6\n166: \n167:     Returns:\n168:         RMS 归一化后的张量，shape 与输入相同\n169: \n170:     Examples:\n171:         >>> x = torch.randn(32, 128, 4096)\n172:         >>> gamma = torch.ones(4096)\n173:         >>> y = rms_norm(x, gamma, epsilon=1e-6)\n174:     \"\"\"\n175:     # 直接调用 PyTorch 原生 RMSNorm 实现；fp16/bf16 输入由 F.rms_norm 内部\n176:     # 自动以 fp32 累加，避免 |x|>256 时 x^2 上溢。\n177:     return torch.nn.functional.rms_norm(\n178:         x, normalized_shape=gamma.shape, weight=gamma, eps=epsilon\n179:     )\n180: ```\n181: \n182: ## 6. 额外信息\n183: \n184: ### 算子调用示例\n185: \n186: ```python\n187: import torch\n188: import cann_bench\n189: \n190: x = torch.randn(32, 128, 4096, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n191: gamma = torch.ones(4096, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n192: \n193: y = cann_bench.rms_norm(x, gamma, epsilon=1e-6)\n194: y = cann_bench.rms_norm(x, gamma, epsilon=1e-5)\n195: ```\n196: \n197: ## proto.yaml\n198: \n199: ```yaml\n200: operator:\n201:   name: RmsNorm\n202:   category: Normalization\n203:   difficulty: L2\n204:   formula: 'y = x / sqrt(mean(x^2) + eps) * gamma\n205: \n206:     '\n207:   description: 计算 RMS (均方根) 归一化\n208:   shape_support: 'x (input): (..., D) - 任意前导维度，最后一维为归一化维度 D\n209: \n210:     gamma (weight): (D,) - 缩放参数，shape 为输入最后一维大小\n211: \n212:     '\n213:   attrs:\n214:   - name: epsilon\n215:     type: float\n216:     default: 1e-6\n217:     description: 数值稳定性参数\n218:     required: false\n219:   inputs:\n220:   - name: x\n221:     description: 输入张量\n222:     dtype:\n223:     - float16\n224:     - float32\n225:     - bfloat16\n226:   - name: gamma\n227:     description: 缩放参数，shape 为输入最后一维大小\n228:     dtype:\n229:     - float16\n230:     - float32\n231:     - bfloat16\n232:   outputs:\n233:   - name: y\n234:     description: RMS 归一化后的张量，与输入 shape 和 dtype 相同\n235:     dtype:\n236:     - float16\n237:     - float32\n238:     - bfloat16\n239:   schema: rms_norm(Tensor x, Tensor gamma, float epsilon=1e-6) -> Tensor y\n240: ```\n241: \n242: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n243: \n244: ```python\n245: #!/usr/bin/python3\n246: # coding=utf-8\n247: \n248: \n249: import torch\n250: \n251: \"\"\"\n252: RmsNorm 算子 Torch Golden 参考实现\n253: \n254: 计算 RMS (均方根) 归一化\n255: \n256: 公式:\n257:     y = x / sqrt(mean(x^2) + eps) * gamma\n258: \n259: 参考 PyTorch API: torch.nn.functional.rms_norm\n260:     https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.rms_norm.html\n261: \n262: 参考论文: Root Mean Square Layer Normalization\n263:     https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07467\n264: \n265: Parameters:\n266:     - x: (..., D) 输入张量，最后一维为归一化维度\n267:     - gamma: (D,) 缩放参数\n268:     - epsilon: float, 默认 1e-6 - 数值稳定性参数\n269: \"\"\"\n270: \n271: \n272: def rms_norm(\n273:     x: torch.Tensor,\n274:     gamma: torch.Tensor,\n275:     epsilon: float = 1e-6\n276: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n277:     \"\"\"\n278:     计算 RMS (均方根) 归一化\n279: \n280:     Args:\n281:         x: 输入张量，shape (..., D)\n282:            最后一维 D 为归一化维度\n283:         gamma: 缩放参数，shape (D,)\n284:                与输入最后一维大小相同\n285:         epsilon: 数值稳定性参数，防止除零\n286:                  默认值 1e-6\n287: \n288:     Returns:\n289:         RMS 归一化后的张量，shape 与输入相同\n290: \n291:     Examples:\n292:         >>> x = torch.randn(32, 128, 4096)\n293:         >>> gamma = torch.ones(4096)\n294:         >>> y = rms_norm(x, gamma, epsilon=1e-6)\n295:     \"\"\"\n296:     # 直接调用 PyTorch 原生 RMSNorm 实现；fp16/bf16 输入由 F.rms_norm 内部\n297:     # 自动以 fp32 累加，避免 |x|>256 时 x^2 上溢。\n298:     return torch.nn.functional.rms_norm(\n299:         x, normalized_shape=gamma.shape, weight=gamma, eps=epsilon\n300:     )\n301: ```\n302: \n303: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n304: \n305: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n306: |---|---|---|---|---|\n307: | 1 | [[32, 128, 768], [768]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [-1, 1] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n308: | 2 | [[32, 128, 1024], [1024]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [-2, 2] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n309: | 3 | [[32, 128, 2048], [2048]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n310: | 4 | [[16, 256, 4096], [4096]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [-10, 10] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n311: | 5 | [[8, 512, 8192], [8192]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [-100, 100] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n312: | 6 | [[4, 1023, 4097], [4097]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [-5, 5] | {'epsilon': 1e-05} |\n313: | 7 | [[63, 67, 1023], [1023]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'epsilon': 1e-08} |\n314: | 8 | [[16, 511, 2049], [2049]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [-1, 1] | {'epsilon': 0.0001} |\n315: | 9 | [[8, 1021, 4099], [4099]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'epsilon': 1e-12} |\n316: | 10 | [[33, 127, 769], [769]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [-1, 2] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n317: | 11 | [[31, 129, 2049], [2049]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [-50, 100] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n318: | 12 | [[17, 255, 4097], [4097]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n319: | 13 | [[7, 1009, 1021], [1021]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [-1, 1] | {'epsilon': 1e-07} |\n320: | 14 | [[11, 367, 373], [373]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [-10, 10] | {'epsilon': 1e-05} |\n321: | 15 | [[1000003, 2], [2]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [None, None] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n322: | 16 | [[11, 13, 17, 67], [67]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [None, None] | {'epsilon': 1e-08} |\n323: | 17 | [[3, 7, 11, 4096], [4096]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [0, 0] | {'epsilon': 0.0001} |\n324: | 18 | [[2, 511, 8192], [8192]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n325: | 19 | [[4, 255, 4096], [4096]] | ['float16', 'float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | {'epsilon': 0.001} |\n326: | 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 128], [128]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [-20, 40] | {'epsilon': 1e-06} |\n327: \n328: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n329: \n330: ```python\n331: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n332: \n333: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n334:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n335:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n336:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n337:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n338:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n339:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n340:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n341:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n342:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n343: \"\"\"\n344: \n345: import torch\n346: \n347: import asc\n348: import asc2\n349: \n350: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n351: \n352: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n353: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n354: _MAX_CORES = 72\n355: \n356: \n357: @asc2.jit\n358: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n359:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n360:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n361:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n362:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n363:                         unroll_factor=2):\n364:         off = t * tile_size\n365:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n366:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n367:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n368:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n369:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n370: \n371: \n372: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n373:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n374:     ensure_npu_platform()\n375:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n376:         x = x.contiguous()\n377:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n378:     size = x.numel()\n379:     if size == 0:\n380:         return out\n381:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n382:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n383:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n384:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n385:     else:\n386:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n387:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n388:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n389:     return out\n390: \n391: ```\n392: \n393: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n394: \n395: ## Module shape\n396: \n397: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n398: \n399: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n400:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n401: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n402: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n403: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n404:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n405:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n406:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n407:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n408: \n409: ## Kernel authoring rules\n410: \n411: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n412:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n413:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n414: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n415:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n416:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n417: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n418: \n419: ```python\n420: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n421:     off = t * tile_size\n422:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n423:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n424:     ...compute on tiles...\n425:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n426: ```\n427: \n428: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n429:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n430: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n431:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n432:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n433:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n434:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n435:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n436:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n437:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n438:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n439:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n440:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n441: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n442:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n443:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n444: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n445:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n446: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n447:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n448: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n449:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n450:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n451:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n452:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n453:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n454:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n455:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n456:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n457:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n458:   cases).\n459: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n460:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n461: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n462:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n463:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n464:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n465:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n466:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n467:   TypeError).\n468: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n469:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n470:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n471:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n472:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n473:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n474:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n475: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n476:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n477:   style (min 32 bytes).\n478: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n479:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n480:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n481: \n482: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n483: \n484: - Never let `exp()` see a positive